            if ((New_Ret < 0) || (New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount) || (New_arrListCount == (Old_arrListCount + 1)) || (New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX))
            {
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$assumption_failure", "Failing assumption on return value, list count or the list contains state for ArrayList.IndexOf");
            }
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is non negative and list count has increased then x can not be in the collection  
            if (!(New_Ret < 0) && (New_arrListCount > Old_arrListCount) && New_arrListContainsX)
            {
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$assumption_failure", "Failing assumption on return value, list count or the list contains state for ArrayList.IndexOf in case list count has increased");
            }	
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If Last index of x is not changed in the same state then x can be found in the list  
            if (New_arrListLastIndexOfX != Old_arrListLastIndexOfX)
            {
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$assumption_failure", "Failing assumption on LastIndexOf x for ArrayList.IndexOf");
            }						
        }
    }
}
```

## PexObserve - Provided by Pex via the PexObserve class

PexObserve is a helper class that outputs the values and exceptions that you generate via System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine. You can also use PexObserve to output a message during the execution of the generated test with WriteLine, Write, or even a custom trace listener. This is a very helpful tool for troubleshooting any problem.

This can be used in this case to validate intermediate states or provide insights into the process:

```csharp
// Example
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
```

## Precondition Assumptions

Precondition assumptions in Pex can be specified using the AssumePrecondition and ExpectException static methods on the PexAssume and PexObserve classes. 

These methods allow you to set a static "precondition assumption" for the values or execution paths represented by your theory points. Assumptions get hoisted out of the code that gets generated and into the main body, meaning that Pex doesn't consider the actual values of these assumptions when it generates tests.

Here is an example where we assume a list count would not be negative:

```csharp
public void SomeMethod([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
{
    // Assume that ArrayList.Count will not return a negative value
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Count >= 0);
    ...
}
```

## In Conclusion

Test methods are methods that cover the contract of a class and validate if the class behaves according to its specification. Pex helps in generating tests for each method, for different execution paths through value and control flow, and for different values of inputs (arguments). It generates a wide variety of possible test sequences based on the static preconditions and exception assumptions you provide. 

However, just due to the number of test inputs there could be, Pex can be slow and it may be hard to debug any issues that it generates or tests. So, it's a great tool but it's not a silver bullet for all problems. It can lead to slow test generation or test execution. Be careful with the number of tests you are running, because it can result in very long debugging times. 

There are also other attributes like [PexInline] that you could use for testing methods on a larger scale too. You would need to generate the tests and then apply the same tests to each of their methods. The key insight here is that the tests must be comprehensive.

This in the context of test driven development (TDD) where tests are written before the actual implementation. But also when you want to ensure that you don't make future changes to the code that could break the existing functionality. The Pex tool can be quite handy in helping to generate those initial tests.

In the case of testing in the real-world application, where you have a lot of moving parts and don't have the luxury of writing all those tests yourself, automated testing tools can come in handy to validate the expected behavior. And--------------------            if (New_Ret < 0)
            {
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
            }

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If x is present in the arrayList, new index of x should not be -1  
	    if (Old_arrListContainsX)
            {
                Assert.AreNotEqual(-1,New_Ret);
            }

