## Invariants

- The array list count remains the same if the return value of IndexOf is negative.
- We keep elements in ArrayList, the position might be changed after Clear, Remove etc. but the elements are all right, and we will find them by IndexOf after.
- Clear the ArrayList then all elements will be removed, the LastIndexOf will return -1, the Contains return false and IndexOf will return -1.
- If the element is not found in the list, LastIndexOf, Contains, and IndexOf should return -1.

- If an element has been added to an empty list then IndexOf returns 0.

- If the same elements are added again and again, then LastIndexOf returns the position of later occurrence.

## Postcondition

- The postcondition for ArrayList.IndexOf tests the return value. If the ArrayList does not contain the specified object, IndexOf returns -1.

## Remarks

- ArrayList.LastIndexOf(object) and ArrayList.Contains(object), which are part of the ArrayList class, are called on the precondition ArrayList as well as on the result of IndexOf.

- The test scenario will create an ArrayList with initial data. And then it will invoke IndexOf method with a random number. For simplicity, let’s assume that the indexed element exists in the list. Pex will try to find the index of this element to test the IndexOf functionality. It will remove a few elements from the list to test the situation after removing elements, including clearing the list and adding new elements back.

- This test suite works correctly when all test scenarios pass. This means that the behavior of ArrayList.IndexOf(object) is correctly implemented. If any of the scenarios fail, you will see an exception, which will prompt you to fix the failing scenario's implementation.

- Because Pex uses the return value of the method under test, the conditions of the precondition and postcondition are designed to ensure that assumptions and assertions are meaningful.

- These test scenarios may not test all the corner cases, it's the responsibility of the programmer to add additional test scenarios as needed for coverage.

The code above is a part of the PexContractTest for the ArrayList class's IndexOf method. This test was performed to ensure that the method IndexOf conforms to the contract provided by the ArrayList class. The test verifies that the ArrayList's behavior is as expected when a new instance of the object under test is created. This test provides a basic level of verification that ensures that IndexOf behaves as expected in a variety of scenarios.

The tests in this test suite check the precondition of IndexOf, if the return value of IndexOf is negative, then the count of ArrayList should be same, if not negative, then it would change. After removing elements from the ArrayList, the LastIndexOf, Contains and IndexOf should return -1, if not, the elements should be kept in ArrayList and could be found by these methods after added back. The postconditions verifies the return value's meaning.

Remember that it's important to consider your specific requirements and add additional test scenarios according to your needs for a complete test. Also, these tests are designed based on certain assumptions, make sure your code doesn't break these assumptions, and make sure these assertions work for your specific situation.

