A: It seems there's a couple of issues with your code:


*

*You're missing the PexMethod attribute on the method. This is required for the Pex to generate test cases for the method.

*You're using the PexInjectRandomTestData attribute, but it's not necessary in this case. Pex generates test cases for the parameters, so you don't need to use the PexInjectRandomTestData attribute.

*You're missing the PexObserve attribute on the "$old_arrList_Count", "$new_arrList_Count", "$old_arrList_x", "$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", "$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", "$New_ret", "$old_arrList_contains_x", and "$new_arrList_contains_x" variables. This attribute is used to observe the values of variables during test execution, but it's not necessary in this case.

*You're using the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute, but it's not necessary in this case. The Pex tool assumes the method under test can throw exceptions, so you don't need to use the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute.

*You have extra blank lines in your code. Remove the blank lines after the "// an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" thatif the return value is negative, list count remains same" comment.


Here's the corrected code:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    PexObserve("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
    PexObserve("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
    PexObserve("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
    PexObserve("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve("$New_ret", New_Ret);
    PexObserve("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
    PexObserve("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);

    if (!New_arrListContainsX)
    {
        Assert.AreEqual(-1, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    }
}

```

After making these changes, the Pex tool should generate test cases for the IndexOf method and execute them properly. The generated test cases will provide input arguments that satisfy the assumptions and assertions in the method.

Let me know if you have any other questions.

Note: Pex requires a .NET Framework 4.5 or later version. If you are using an older version of the framework, you may need to upgrade.


